    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [x] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version of the `main` branch\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.files\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.12.x\n\n### Description\n\nThe documentation has instruction on how to toggle the explorer:\n\nhttps://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/946ae64e0ee807ae3c41f382f0114b4ed4915b2c/lua/mini/files.lua#L382-L389\n\nThis works fine most of the time, but when I try to toggle off the modified explorer (e.g., a new file has been created) without synchronization and answer \"No\" to the confirmation nessage, it seems to invoke `MiniFiles.open` and `MiniFiles.close` one more time; I get the confirmation message twice when using the following keymap:\n\n```lua\nvim.keymap.set('n', '-', function()\n  if not MiniFiles.close() then\n    MiniFiles.open()\n  end\nend)\n```\n\nThings get worse when I have `MiniFiles.open(vim.api.nvim_buf_get_name(0))`, as it eventually tries to do `MiniFiles.open(\"minifiles:/N/path/to/some/file\")` (See the reproduction steps).\n\nFWIW, the default `q` keybinding does not have this issue, so I expect this toggling logic should also work as `q` when closing the explorer.\n\n### Reproduction\n\n1. Create separate 'nvim-repro' config directory:\n    - '~/.config/nvim-repro/' on Unix\n    - '~/AppData/Local/nvim-repro/' on Windows\n\n2. Inside 'nvim-repro' directory create a file named 'init.lua'.\n   Populate it with the following content:\n\n    ```lua\n    vim.pack.add({ 'https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.files' })\n\n    local files = require('mini.files')\n\n    vim.keymap.set('n', '-', function()\n      if not files.close() then\n        files.open(vim.api.nvim_buf_get_name(0))\n      end\n    end)\n    ```\n\n3. Run `NVIM_APPNAME=nvim-repro nvim` (i.e. execute `nvim` with `NVIM_APPNAME` environment variable set to \"nvim-repro\").\n   Wait for all dependencies to install.\n\n4. Replace this with description of interactive reproduction steps along with the behavior you observe.\n   1. Open the explorer using the keybinding `-`.\n   2. Add a new file (i.e., add a newline and write an arbitrary file name to the explorer buffer).\n   3. Try to close the explorer using the keybinding `-`.\n   4. The confirm message pops up:\n      ```\n      There are pending file system actions\n\n      Close without synchronization?\n      [Y]es, (N)o:\n      ```\n   5. Press `n`.\n   6. The following error message pops up:\n      ```\n      E5108: Lua: (mini.files) `path` is not a valid path (\"minifiles:/2/home/test/.config/nvim-repro\")\n      stack traceback:\n              [C]: in function 'error'\n              ...m-repro/site/pack/core/opt/mini.files/lua/mini/files.lua:3063: in function 'error'\n              ...m-repro/site/pack/core/opt/mini.files/lua/mini/files.lua:775: in function 'open'\n              /home/test/.config/nvim-repro/init.lua:7: in function </home/test/.config/nvim-repro/init.lua:5>\n      ```\n   7. The stack trace shows that (somehow) I must have invoked `MiniFiles.open` with `\"minifiles:/2/home/test/.config/nvim-repro\"`, which is unexpected.\n\n<details><summary>What to do after reporting an issue</summary>\n\nAfter reporting the issue, it is safe (and even recommended for cleaner possible future bug reports) to remove 'nvim-repro' config from the system:\n- Delete config directory ('~/.config/nvim-repro' on Unix).\n- Delete data directory ('~/.local/share/nvim-repro' on Unix).\n- Delete state directory ('~/.local/state/nvim-repro' on Unix).\n\n</details>",
